  "response": "The scanner flagged `src/assets/check-icon.png` as **crime_00014** (\"Icon-Sized Raster\"), and the evidence describes its dimensions verbatim as:\n\n> \"dimensions: 32 × 32 px (≤ 64 threshold both sides)\"\n\nAt 32×32 px, a new PNG indicator is a bad starting point for three reasons that flow directly from that finding:\n\n1. **It's locked to one resolution.** The scanner notes the asset \"locks the image to a single resolution\" — on a 2x or 3x display the 32×32 PNG either renders soft or forces shipping `@2x`/`@3x` variants. An SVG renders crisply at every DPI from one file.\n\n2. **Icon-sized geometry is exactly what SVG was built for.** The detector's threshold (`≤ 64` on both sides) exists precisely because small UI glyphs — checks, status dots, indicators — are almost always simple paths that compress smaller as vector than as raster. The suggested action says it directly: \"same render at every DPI, smaller bytes.\"\n\n3. **Copying the pattern propagates the crime.** If you mirror `check-icon.png` for the new indicator, you've now got two icon-sized rasters instead of one, and the next person adding a third status will follow the same precedent. Starting the new indicator as SVG breaks that chain and gives you a vector source the existing check icon can eventually migrate toward.\n\nBottom line: the existing 32×32 PNG is already a known low-severity crime; ship the new indicator as SVG and don't inherit the resolution lock.",
